[CONFIRM] lead 2 - shape B - confidence LOW
  claim:             The local OAuth 'authorization code' setup scripts scripts/gmail-oauth.ts and scripts/outlook-oauth.ts build the provider authorization URL and run a localhost callback HTTP server, but never generate, send, or verify an OAuth `state` parameter — the standard CSRF/authorization-code-injection protection for OAuth flows is absent.
  lead evidence:     In scripts/gmail-oauth.ts, the authUrl.searchParams.set(...) sequence (lines 34-39) sets client_id, redirect_uri, response_type, scope, access_type, prompt — no 'state'. The createServer callback handler (lines 45+) reads only `code` from url.searchParams and exchanges it for a token, with no state check. scripts/outlook-oauth.ts (lines 34-47+) follows the identical pattern (client_id, redirect_uri, etc., and a callback reading only `code`/`error`). A targeted Cypher query `WHERE (c.file_path='scripts/gmail-oauth.ts' OR c.file_path='scripts/outlook-oauth.ts') AND toLower(c.code) CONTAINS 'state'` returned row_count: 0, confirming no `state` parameter is generated, sent, or validated in either file.
  verdict reason:    Read both files in full at /home/lutherleo/apex/scripts/gmail-oauth.ts and scripts/outlook-oauth.ts. Both build an authorization URL with searchParams.set() calls (client_id, redirect_uri, response_type, scope, plus access_type/prompt in gmail or response_mode in outlook) — no 'state' parameter is ever set. Both spin up a local http.createServer callback handler that reads only `code` (and `error` in the outlook variant) from url.searchParams, with zero comparison against any stored/expected state value, before POSTing the code straight to the token endpoint. This is the standard OAuth authorization-code-flow CSRF/injection gap (RFC 6749 §10.12): an attacker can trigger the victim's browser to hit http://localhost:8457/callback?code=<attacker_code>, and the script will exchange the attacker's authorization code and print/save the resulting tokens as if they were the user's own, with no state check to detect the request wasn't the one the script itself initiated. Independently confirmed with my own Cypher query (not reusing the analyst's numbers blindly, just its query shape) against scan_id 69dd1f145c434646e9d435aecf42ddb74e5e28c5, which also returned row_count 0 for a 'state' substring search across both files, matching my direct source read. Line numbers I observed (gmail: searchParams.set block lines 34-39, callback at 45-108; outlook: searchParams.set block lines 37-41, callback at 47-130) are consistent with the analyst's cited ranges. Note: these are one-time developer/testing helper scripts (not production entry points), which limits real-world blast radius — the analyst's LOW confidence rating is appropriate — but the described code defect (missing OAuth state) is real and accurately characterized.
  verifier evidence: /home/lutherleo/apex/scripts/gmail-oauth.ts lines 33-39 (authUrl.searchParams.set: client_id, redirect_uri, response_type, scope, access_type, prompt — no state) and lines 45-59 (callback handler reads only `code`, no state check); /home/lutherleo/apex/scripts/outlook-oauth.ts lines 34-41 (same pattern, no state) and lines 47-77 (callback reads `code`/`error` only, no state check); Cypher query `MATCH (c:CpgCall) WHERE c.scan_id='69dd1f145c434646e9d435aecf42ddb74e5e28c5' AND (c.file_path='scripts/gmail-oauth.ts' OR c.file_path='scripts/outlook-oauth.ts') AND toLower(c.code) CONTAINS 'state' RETURN ...` → row_count: 0.

[CONFIRM] lead 3 - shape C - confidence MEDIUM
  claim:             In src/core/agents/offSecAgent/tools/email/sendEmail.ts (~line 129-137), the nodemailer transport is created with `tls: smtp.tls ? { rejectUnauthorized: false } : undefined` — whenever TLS is enabled for the configured SMTP server, certificate validation is explicitly and unconditionally disabled, allowing a network MITM to intercept or tamper with outbound emails (and any credentials/tokens they contain) without the client ever noticing an invalid certificate.
  lead evidence:     Query: MATCH (c:CpgCall) WHERE c.scan_id=$scan_id AND toLower(c.code) CONTAINS 'rejectunauthorized' RETURN c.file_path, c.line, c.code — returned exactly one real code site: src/core/agents/offSecAgent/tools/email/sendEmail.ts line 129/137, code `tls: smtp.tls ? { rejectUnauthorized: false } : undefined` inside `createTransport({...})`. Cross-checked this file is also a live EntryPoint: MATCH (ep:EntryPoint)-[:ENTERS_AT]->(m:CpgMethod) WHERE toLower(m.file_path) CONTAINS 'sendemail' returned ep.method_full_name = 'src/core/agents/offSecAgent/tools/email/sendEmail.ts::program:sendEmail' with exposure='reachable', kind='handler'. All other 'rejectUnauthorized'/'InsecureSkipVerify' hits in the graph (9 total) were confirmed to be documentation/pattern strings inside src/core/whitebox/profiles.ts (a static list of regex patterns used for scanning other codebases), not live code.
  verdict reason:    Read the real source at /home/lutherleo/apex/src/core/agents/offSecAgent/tools/email/sendEmail.ts and confirmed line 137 sets `tls: smtp.tls ? { rejectUnauthorized: false } : undefined` inside the `createTransport({...})` call passed to nodemailer (lines 129-138). This means whenever an operator enables TLS for the configured SMTP relay (smtp.tls truthy), certificate validation (`rejectUnauthorized`) is explicitly forced to false with no override/opt-out, disabling Node's TLS chain/hostname verification for that connection — a textbook MITM-enabling misconfiguration (no CVE needed; this is direct API misuse of Node's tls option, well documented as dangerous). Independently ran the analyst's cypher queries against scan_id 69dd1f145c434646e9d435aecf42ddb74e5e28c5: the `rejectunauthorized` code-content query returns exactly 9 rows — 6 are the live call-site fragments at sendEmail.ts:129/137 matching the source verbatim, and the other 3 are regex pattern-string literals inside src/core/whitebox/profiles.ts (a static scanning-rules list, not executable logic reachable at runtime). The EntryPoint query confirms sendEmail.ts::program:sendEmail (and its nested :execute) are registered EntryPoints with exposure='reachable', kind='handler', so this code path is live/reachable, not dead code. No compensating control (e.g., a strict-mode override, custom CA pinning, or restricting this to a local/dev-only server) is present anywhere in the file.
